WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.160
Peace be upon you and welcome to the second module of our fellowship.

00:04.520 --> 00:08.440
I am glad that you completed the first module and that you are continuing with us here.

00:08.520 --> 00:13.680
In this explanation today, we are before environmental health that will completely change our view of human health.

00:13.820 --> 00:20.380
We begin our journey that will move us from merely the concept of individual medical care inside the hospital to a concept much wider and more important:

00:20.760 --> 00:23.020
protecting and defending the community as a whole.

00:23.480 --> 00:25.660
There is an important question that always poses itself:

00:25.660 --> 00:30.660
Imagine if we built the best and most luxurious hospital in the world, would people stop falling ill?

00:31.180 --> 00:39.220
Let us imagine a scene together: an imaginary hospital containing the latest medical devices in the world and the cleverest doctors.

00:39.340 --> 00:47.780
Yet despite all this grandeur, the emergency room is still full, waiting halls are crowded all year round, and the counter of patients is increasing, not decreasing.

00:47.780 --> 00:53.780
Is the solution to build another hospital, buy more devices, and bring in more doctors?

00:55.660 --> 01:01.740
Or is there another energy and power we can use to protect them before the disease knocks on their door in the first place?

01:01.740 --> 01:04.660
The answer simply takes us to the core of public health.

01:05.400 --> 01:08.160
The idea here is not merely a complicated academic term;

01:08.500 --> 01:12.740
no, this is the art and science that protects the health of entire communities as a single block.

01:13.140 --> 01:15.800
The hospital looks at the patient as an individual needing treatment,

01:16.140 --> 01:18.680
yet public health looks at the community as a whole.

01:18.680 --> 01:23.660
It is what goes down to the street, moves a step ahead, builds a safety valve, and prevents disease from the threshold,

01:23.660 --> 01:27.040
instead of spending millions on medications after the damage is already done.

01:27.260 --> 01:30.560
To bring the picture closer, let us enter the first section:

01:30.900 --> 01:31.900
The story of the hundred children,

01:32.200 --> 01:35.960
and how it summarizes the difference between medical care and public health.

01:35.960 --> 01:39.600
We have a full school with one hundred children playing happily in safety.

01:39.600 --> 01:42.900
If one child among them, God forbid, contracts an infectious disease like measles,

01:42.900 --> 01:47.320
the clinical doctor steps in at their clinic to diagnose them and write them medication until they recover.

01:47.320 --> 01:50.620
And this, of course, is a great and important human role that we cannot do without.

01:50.620 --> 01:52.060
Yet the fundamental question here:

01:52.060 --> 01:55.560
What is the condition of the other ninety-nine children who are still sitting playing?

01:55.560 --> 01:57.860
How do we ensure that infection does not reach them?

01:57.860 --> 02:02.760
And how do we protect them fundamentally so they continue playing without fear and not need to go to the doctor?

02:02.760 --> 02:06.080
Right here appears the magic and miracle of public health.

02:06.080 --> 02:09.880
We are talking about tools that act like a protective shield covering the entire school

02:09.880 --> 02:11.880
and protecting all the children at the exact same moment.

02:11.880 --> 02:15.780
Things like vaccination programs, awareness of personal hygiene,

02:15.780 --> 02:18.980
and designing a fundamentally healthy and safe school environment.

02:18.980 --> 02:25.320
All these are tools operating silently to preserve the health of the remaining children and completely prevent the spread of disease.

02:25.320 --> 02:27.440
And this takes us to the second section:

02:27.440 --> 02:31.140
Comparison between individual healthcare and public health.

02:31.140 --> 02:34.380
There is a big confusion between the two in our daily lives.

02:34.380 --> 02:38.040
Healthcare focuses on the individual: a patient in pain,

02:38.040 --> 02:40.340
so the doctor deals with their symptoms and treats them.

02:40.340 --> 02:44.140
While public health stands on the hill, its eyes capturing the entire landscape,

02:44.140 --> 02:47.480
asking the most important question: Why did this disease appear in the first place?

02:47.480 --> 02:48.980
And how do we change the environment?

02:48.980 --> 02:52.580
And eliminate its causes to reduce the numbers of patients in the community?

02:52.580 --> 02:56.600
And the beauty here is that genuine public health functions as an invisible shield in our lives:

02:56.600 --> 02:59.800
The clean water we drink every day without thinking about it,

02:59.800 --> 03:02.000
the vaccines we received when we were small,

03:02.000 --> 03:07.340
and even smoking ban regulations in public places that protect our lungs daily without us feeling it.

03:07.340 --> 03:11.040
And now in the third section, we connect two points of utmost importance:

03:11.040 --> 03:13.540
Climate action and community health.

03:13.540 --> 03:17.140
The greatest challenge facing the shield of public health today is climate change.

03:17.140 --> 03:20.760
Imagine a severe and unprecedented heatwave striking the city:

03:20.760 --> 03:24.360
Hospitals prepare to receive heat exhaustion cases,

03:24.360 --> 03:27.200
and doctors begin administering intravenous fluids to patients.

03:27.200 --> 03:31.840
Yet the public health system does not wait for people to collapse, go to the hospital, and fill the beds.

03:31.840 --> 03:35.400
Public health moves immediately with a fast and proactive plan:

03:35.400 --> 03:38.800
First, it warns field workers on the streets early,

03:38.800 --> 03:41.800
prepares and opens air-conditioned cooling centers for the public,

03:41.800 --> 03:45.100
and checks on the elderly in their homes to protect them.

03:45.100 --> 03:49.160
In these moments, awareness of climate hazards ceases to be merely theoretical talk;

03:49.160 --> 03:51.600
it transforms into a protective shield saving lives.

03:51.600 --> 03:55.000
Protecting the environment and protecting people's health are two faces of the same coin,

03:55.000 --> 04:00.600
and here climate science unites with community health to create genuine difference and impact.

04:00.600 --> 04:05.200
We reach the fourth section: Voices of the community and the behind-the-scenes champions.

04:05.200 --> 04:08.800
Who brings these words down to the ground and makes them a lived reality?

04:08.800 --> 04:11.500
They are the community health educators.

04:11.500 --> 04:16.900
Their role is not just to write a prescription or hold a surgeon's scalpel; their role is far more powerful.

04:16.900 --> 04:22.300
They are the ones who translate complex science into simple steps helping families make healthy decisions every day.

04:22.300 --> 04:28.600
They are the true connecting link that carries us from mere medical data to actual, sustainable safety on the ground.

04:28.600 --> 04:31.500
Therefore, we conclude this explanation with a simple challenge:

04:31.500 --> 04:37.400
Think of a daily habit you practice, such as drinking clean water or avoiding going out during peak heat:

04:37.400 --> 04:42.700
How can this simple individual habit transform into a shield protecting an entire community?

04:42.700 --> 04:46.700
Thinking about the answer to this question will make us realize the profound impact of our simple actions.

04:46.700 --> 04:55.500
In the upcoming video, we will delve deeper into the story to see how climate change specifically poses a direct threat to public health in our Arab region,

04:55.500 --> 05:00.300
and how we translate this understanding into field steps on the ground.

05:00.300 --> 05:03.700
Thank you for your attention, and we will see you in the next video, God willing.
